Aim: To explore and practice basic Docker commands for managing containers and images.

Requirements:
- Docker
- Ubuntu terminal
- Internet connection
Step 1: Check Docker version
docker --version


Output:
Docker version ...


Step 2: Check Docker information
docker info


Displays information about Docker, containers, images, and storage.
Step 3: Download an image
docker pull hello-world


Output:
Status: Downloaded newer image for hello-world


Step 4: Display Docker images
docker images


Output:
REPOSITORY     TAG       IMAGE ID
hello-world    latest    xxxxx


Step 5: Run a container
docker run hello-world


Output:
Hello from Docker!
This message shows that your installation appears to be working correctly.


Step 6: Run an interactive Ubuntu container
docker run -it ubuntu bash


Inside the container:
echo "Welcome to Docker"


Output:
Welcome to Docker


Exit the container:
exit


Step 7: Display running containers
docker ps


Displays currently running containers.

To display all containers:
docker ps -a


Step 8: Start a container
docker start container_id


Step 9: Stop a container
docker stop container_id


Step 10: Restart a container
docker restart container_id


Step 11: Remove a container
docker rm container_id


Step 12: Remove an image
docker rmi hello-world


Step 13: View container logs
docker logs container_id


Step 14: View container processes
docker top container_id


Step 15: Inspect a container
docker inspect container_id


Important Docker commands
Command	Description
docker pull	Download an image
docker images	List images
docker run	Create and run a container
docker ps	List running containers
docker ps -a	List all containers
docker start	Start a stopped container
docker stop	Stop a running container
docker restart	Restart a container
docker rm	Remove a container
docker rmi	Remove an image
docker logs	Display container logs
docker exec -it	Execute commands inside a running container

Result: Successfully explored and executed basic Docker commands for managing images and containers.
